// **This game's policy over the shared library.**
//
// `library.ron` holds measurements: what the mesh is, how big it is, which way it faces. Those are
// true in any game that loads the asset. This file holds the things that are true *here* — SCP-9191's
// architecture, its ceiling height, the height it hangs a sign at. Baking those into the library is
// how one game's wall height ends up in another game's rooms with the library file as the only
// evidence.
//
// Each patch names either one descriptor id or one `kind` token, and says **why**. Rules apply in the
// order written and the last word wins, so this reads top to bottom.
//
//   ( match: Kind("door"),   because: "...", patch: ( align: ( stretch_y: Some(1.2) ) ) )
//   ( match: Id("wall_light"), because: "...", patch: ( mount: Some(OnWall(( height: 2.0 ))) ) )
//
// It is currently empty, and that is a statement rather than a placeholder: everything in the emerge
// library today is used at its measured size. The Site kit is the set that carries real policy — its
// walls are scaled to `WALL_HEIGHT` 2.4 m and its doorframes to `DOORWAY_HEIGHT` 2.0 m, both derived
// numbers about this facility — and when those pieces migrate to descriptors, their scaling belongs
// here rather than in the measurements.
(
    version: 1,
    note: Some("SCP-9191 containment site. Nothing in the emerge library is re-proportioned for this facility yet; the Site kit's 2.4 m walls and 2.0 m doorways land here when those pieces migrate."),
    // How finely each authoring tile divides, for every piece's lattice. 1 means a subunit is
    // `grid::SNAP` itself -- the half-metre grid these kits are already authored on, on which a
    // 3 m wall is 6 divisions across and a 2.4 m one is 5 layers tall. Raising it refines every
    // piece in the project by the same factor, which is what keeps two faces comparable.
    face_bands: 1,
    patches: [],
)
